segment_7d.text:
The terminal opens a carrier shell and fails to render most of it.

{corrupt:0.3:random}SIGNAL ORIGIN: // RELAY INDEX MISSING // CARRIER: CLASS-7 BURST // ENCODING: UNDECLARED // RECIPIENT FIELD UNAVAILABLE // AWAITING RESPONSE{/corrupt}

At first glance it is ordinary damage: compression noise, storage rot, a packet old enough to have lost its edges. You log the integrity percentage and prepare the standard recovery procedure.

partial_decode.text:
The field resolves:

{corrupt:0.85:consistent}SIGNAL ORIGIN: —— CARRIER: CLASS-7 BURST — ENCODING: UNKNOWN —{/corrupt}
DESIGNATED RECIPIENT: {player_name}
{corrupt:0.85:consistent}—— TIMESTAMP: —— AWAITING RESPONSE{/corrupt}

You do not touch the keyboard for three recycler clicks.

The terminal has no reason to know that name. The archive has less reason.
